# RED proof — the options-shape drift test against the pre-work server

Task: t_f881bb6a (PLAN-0006 Track D). Test: "RED: both order schemas declare
items[].options as group id -> array of choice ids, and the server accepts it"
(services/restaurant-cvm/server_test.ts).

Method: `git checkout 7aa2829 -- services/restaurant-cvm/server.ts
tools/venue_to_announcement.ts` (the branch base, i.e. the server BEFORE this
work), keep the new test file, then run:

    deno test --no-check --allow-read services/restaurant-cvm/server_test.ts \
      --filter "both order schemas declare items"

(--no-check because `deno test` type-checks, and the pre-work sources do not carry
the option types the new test file imports; the type error is not the RED signal.)

Result — failed, for the intended reason (the announced schema carried no typed
`options` property at all):

    error: TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'items')
        }).properties.items.items.properties.options;
                      ^
        at .../services/restaurant-cvm/server_test.ts:910:19

     FAILURES
    RED: both order schemas declare items[].options as group id -> array of
    choice ids, and the server accepts it => ./services/restaurant-cvm/server_test.ts:889:6

    FAILED | 0 passed | 1 failed | 43 filtered out (328ms)

Then the pre-work sources were restored and the full suite run green
(97 passed | 0 failed | 1 ignored; see REPORT.md for the run).

Why this test exists at all: the older drift test compares the announced and the
served order schema to EACH OTHER, so both sides can move together to a bare
`{type: "object"}` and still pass. This one asserts the shape on both sides AND
that the server accepts the shape its own schema declares.
